# Rest Thermostat (Expo) — example environment file.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (`.env` is gitignored). NOTHING IN THIS
# FILE IS REQUIRED — the app is fully configurable through the
# in-app onboarding flow. These variables only exist as a
# developer convenience to pre-fill the onboarding screens when
# running the app locally against a known backend.
#
# Two prefix conventions are used (see CLAUDE.md §4):
#
#   * `EXPO_PUBLIC_*`  — bundled into the JS shipped to end users.
#                        Safe ONLY for non-secret values (a server
#                        URL is fine; a service-token secret is not,
#                        unless you accept that the secret will be
#                        readable in your release binary).
#   * (bare name)      — only available at build time / EAS scripts,
#                        never imported by client code.
#
# After editing this file, restart `npx expo start` — Expo only
# reads env vars at process start.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dev seeding (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of your NoLongerEvil server. Accepts bare host, host:port,
# or full URL. Examples:
#   http://192.168.1.50:8082
#   https://nle.example.com
# Leave unset to enter the URL through the onboarding screen instead.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_NLE_BASE_URL=

# Cloudflare Access service token, used when your NLE server sits
# behind Cloudflare Access. Obtain via:
#   https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/identity/service-tokens/
# These values WILL be bundled into your local JS bundle if you set
# them here — only do this on a development machine.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build-time secrets (NEVER prefixed with EXPO_PUBLIC_)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Used by EAS Build to sign releases. Prefer `eas secret:create` over
# setting these locally so they never sit in plaintext on disk.
# EAS_BUILD_TOKEN=

# Sentry DSN, if you wire up crash reporting in your fork. The DSN
# itself is generally considered semi-public, but treat it as a
# secret if your Sentry project has organization-level scopes.
# SENTRY_DSN=
